[size=5][b]Fixes in this release[/b][/size]

Where a fix was sourced from, or corroborated against, another modder's work, that is recorded per fix in the Credit and sources section below.

[size=4][b]Performance[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Fix Portal Connection Scan[/b] [i](server)[/i] — pairs portals through a tag index instead of rescanning every portal for each unconnected one every five seconds.
[*][b]Fix World Load Connection Scan[/b] [i](server)[/i] — indexes the world-load pairing of portals, spawners and sync transforms with their targets instead of comparing every pair.
[*][b]Fix Disconnect ZDO Sweep[/b] [i](server)[/i] — indexes non-persistent objects by owner, so a disconnect sweeps only the departing player's objects instead of every ZDO in the world.
[*][b]Fix Tar Pit Buffer Disposal[/b] [i](client)[/i] — disposes tar pit raycast buffers only when they were allocated, so a tar pit whose load failed cannot throw out of scene teardown.
[*][b]Fix ZDO Packet Allocation[/b] [i](both)[/i] — writes one network package into another straight from its buffer instead of copying the whole payload onto the heap first.
[*][b]Fix Mist Query Overhead[/b] [i](client)[/i] — answers mist volume queries from a zone-bucketed snapshot and heightmap data instead of scanning every mist volume with native reads and physics rays per particle.
[*][b]Fix Heightmap Lookup Scan[/b] [i](both)[/i] — finds the terrain tile under a point with a zone-keyed lookup instead of scanning every loaded tile.
[*][b]Fix Static Object Ground Checks[/b] [i](both)[/i] — reads a tree or rock's position once per ground check instead of about six times.
[*][b]Fix Grass Rebuild Burst[/b] [i](client)[/i] — spreads a whole-area grass rebuild over a few frames, nearest patches first, instead of regenerating every patch in one frame.
[*][b]Fix Terrain Builder Throughput[/b] [i](both)[/i] — makes the terrain build thread sleep only when idle and hold more finished results.
[*][b]Fix Zone Collider Stall[/b] [i](client)[/i] — bakes the terrain collider of an already-generated zone on a background thread instead of the main thread.
[*][b]Fix Prefab Query Scan[/b] [i](both)[/i] — answers "every object of this prefab" from an index instead of scanning every ZDO in the world.
[*][b]Fix Grass Ground Raycasts[/b] [i](client)[/i] — reads the ground height, slope and biome for grass placement from terrain data instead of casting a physics ray per blade.
[*][b]Fix Background Zone Pacing[/b] [i](server)[/i] — defers background zone pre-generation by a tick when the previous frame ran long or the last generation was expensive (both configurable).
[*][b]Fix Water Material Lookup[/b] [i](client)[/i] — caches each water tile's surface material instead of fetching it from the engine every frame.
[*][b]Fix Distant Terrain Hitch[/b] [i](client)[/i] — rebuilds the far-terrain ring a few tiles per frame (configurable; 9 is vanilla) instead of all nine at once.
[*][b]Fix Idle Scene Sweep[/b] [i](both)[/i] — skips the 30 Hz object create/destroy pass when cheap change-tracking proves nothing changed, keeping one full pass per second as a safety sweep.
[*][b]Fix Support Lookup Cost[/b] [i](both)[/i] — resolves which building piece owns a collider through a lookup table instead of a hierarchy walk per collider.
[*][b]Fix Light Flicker Overhead[/b] [i](client)[/i] — stops updating torch flicker for lights past both a configurable distance and their own light LOD distance.
[*][b]Fix Piece Event Stall[/b] [i](both)[/i] — registers building pieces for terrain-rebuild cache clears in a per-heightmap table instead of an event whose subscribe copies and unsubscribe scans the whole list.
[*][b]Fix Unload Sweep Cost[/b] [i](both)[/i] — runs the object-unload sweep on a configurable wall-clock interval (default 100 ms) instead of on every pass.
[*][b]Fix Spawn Queue Churn[/b] [i](both)[/i] — keeps the sorted spawn backlog between frames instead of rebuilding and re-sorting it thirty times a second.
[*][b]Fix Zone Occupancy Scan[/b] [i](both)[/i] — answers whether a zone still holds objects from a per-zone tally instead of walking every loaded object.
[*][b]Fix Piece Material Polling[/b] [i](both)[/i] — waits for a piece's random material seed on one shared ticker and stops polling once the values are applied.
[*][b]Fix Idle Support Checks[/b] [i](both)[/i] — lets a building piece skip its structural support re-check until a neighbour is built, destroyed or changed or the terrain is edited; "Support Change Threshold" and "Settled Piece Patience" tune how a neighbour's small drift wakes it.
[*][b]Fix Smoke Overhead[/b] [i](client)[/i] — writes each smoke puff's physics mass on 2% lifetime steps, rechecks its render chunk four times a second, and reads its position once per frame.
[*][b]Fix Unload Discovery Scan[/b] [i](both)[/i] — finds objects that left the loaded area through a per-zone instance index instead of walking every loaded object; "Object Unload Frame Budget" caps how many are handed to the engine per pass (default 250, 0 is vanilla).
[*][b]Fix Idle Wear Visits[/b] [i](both)[/i] — skips a building piece's whole wear visit while it is provably quiet: support asleep, locally owned, dry or roofed while wet, above the waterline, outside the Ashlands, and undamaged since the last visit.
[*][b]Fix ZDO Value Write Allocation[/b] [i](both)[/i] — compares a ZDO field write against its stored value without boxing it.
[*][b]Fix Doubled ZDO Lookups[/b] [i](both)[/i] — reads ZDO data with one dictionary lookup instead of two.
[*][b]Fix Collision Contact Allocation[/b] [i](both)[/i] — reads a collision's contact points into a reused buffer instead of allocating an array per collision callback.
[*][b]Fix Collision Callback Allocation[/b] [i](both)[/i] — turns on Unity's [font=Courier New]reuseCollisionCallbacks[/font] so one collision object serves every callback; the first suspect if a physics-touching mod that stores collision objects misbehaves.
[*][b]Fix Equipment Visual Refresh[/b] [i](client)[/i] — re-applies a character's skin and hair colour only when an input changed, and reads its equipment fields with one table lookup instead of thirty.
[*][b]Fix Light Settings Subscription[/b] [i](client)[/i] — registers lights for graphics-setting changes in a lookup table instead of a static event whose unsubscribe scans every other lit light.
[*][b]Fix Portal Idle Updates[/b] [i](client)[/i] — stops a portal re-writing its emission colour, light and audio every frame once the connection fade has finished.
[*][b]Fix Material Fader Settling[/b] [i](client)[/i] — stops a finished fade re-applying the same material property block to every renderer every frame; lingering corpses are the common case.
[*][b]Fix Cooking Slot Keys[/b] [i](both)[/i] — reads and writes a cooking station's slot data through precomputed key hashes instead of building six strings per access, as [font=Courier New]ArmorStand[/font] already does.
[*][b]Fix Station Range Scans[/b] [i](client)[/i] — answers the per-frame build-mode station and extension range queries with squared distances and one position read per candidate instead of two.
[*][b]Fix Smelter Catch-up Reads[/b] [i](both)[/i] — memoises a smelter's fuel and ore reads against its data revision, so an hour of missed production is not re-read once per simulated second.
[*][b]Fix Idle Sound Updates[/b] [i](client)[/i] — drops a finished one-shot sound out of the per-frame audio updater list until something plays it again.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Terrain[/b][/size]

[b]Fix Terrain Seams[/b] and [b]Fix Terrain Paint Seams[/b] change only what is drawn, never what is saved, so they are client-only. [b]Fix Terrain Paint Zone Fanout[/b] changes what is recorded, so it runs on every side.

[list]
[*][b]Fix Terrain Seams[/b] [i](client)[/i] — computes terrain lighting normals across zone boundaries, removing the hard crease along the 64 m grid.
[*][b]Fix Terrain Paint Seams[/b] [i](client)[/i] — merges the two copies of the paint on a zone boundary so dirt continues across the line instead of stopping dead; a lone stripe on the border is dropped.
[*][b]Fix Terrain Paint Zone Fanout[/b] [i](both)[/i] — records terrain paint into every zone it actually covers, including the neighbour about a metre west or south that vanilla left out; affects edits made from now on.
[*][b]Fix Terrain Paint Mask Indexing[/b] [i](client)[/i] — corrects the stride and bounds the [font=Courier New]optterrain[/font] console command uses to walk terrain paint data.
[*][b]Fix Terrain Compiler Init Race[/b] [i](both)[/i] — recovers a zone's terrain compiler that loaded before its heightmap existed, instead of throwing every frame and ignoring edits.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Correctness[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Fix Object Unload Crash[/b] [i](both)[/i] — recovers from an orphaned scene instance during object unloading instead of aborting the pass every frame.
[*][b]Tolerate Duplicate ZDOs On Load[/b] [i](server)[/i] — loads a save containing duplicate ZDO ids, keeping the later one and logging a warning, instead of refusing to load.
[*][b]Fix Effect Areas[/b] [i](both)[/i] — grows the fixed 128-collider buffer behind fire warmth and wetness checks when it fills, and removes destroyed characters from effect areas instead of throwing every physics step.
[*][b]Fix Fuel And Ore Loss[/b] [i](client)[/i] — takes ownership of a smelter, kiln or fireplace before adding fuel or ore, so the item cannot be lost to a dropped network message.
[*][b]Refund Rejected Station Items[/b] [i](both)[/i] — drops an item back at the player when a smelter, kiln, fireplace, cooking station or fermenter discards it on arrival because the station changed owner or filled up after the item left their inventory; covers a vanilla sender and the "last slot" race.
[*][b]Share Boss Defeat Keys[/b] [i](client)[/i] — gives every nearby player the per-player boss defeat key, not only the one whose client owned the boss; works against a vanilla server.
[*][b]Fix Recipe Amount Crash[/b] [i](client)[/i] — guards the null dereference that broke the crafting panel for a "requires any one of these" recipe with none of the ingredients carried.
[*][b]Fix Spawner Null Prefabs[/b] [i](both)[/i] — drops null entries from a spawner's creature table on load instead of letting one kill the spawner.
[*][b]Fix Projectile Rotation Spam[/b] [i](both)[/i] — stops the [font=Courier New]Look rotation viewing vector is zero[/font] log line that a projectile at zero velocity writes every physics step.
[*][b]Fix Send Failure Log Spam[/b] [i](both)[/i] — redirects the per-frame [font=Courier New]Failed to send data[/font] log line to debug logging.
[*][b]Fix Container Log Spam[/b] [i](both)[/i] — redirects the four log lines written on every chest open, stack-all and take-all to debug logging.
[*][b]Fix Item Icon Crash[/b] [i](client)[/i] — draws the first icon for an item whose stored icon variant is out of range instead of throwing from every UI panel.
[*][b]Fix Negative Stamina[/b] [i](client)[/i] — floors player stamina at zero ([font=Courier New]NaN[/font] included), repairs a character that loads in broken, and drops a [font=Courier New]UseStamina[/font] network message carrying [font=Courier New]NaN[/font] or infinity.
[*][b]Fix Dungeon Load Stall[/b] [i](both)[/i] — counts a dungeon room whose asset failed to load as finished and drops it, so the zone is not left flagged as loading forever.
[*][b]Fix Teleport Ghost Players[/b] [i](server)[/i] — tells a client to drop a player who teleported out of its loaded area, instead of leaving them standing frozen where they left (and still in local chat range).
[*][b]Fix Unsaved Client Changes[/b] [i](server)[/i] — marks an object a connected player placed or changed for the next world save; the chunked save format only rewrites chunks the game marked as changed, and it never marks one for a change that arrives from another player.
[*][b]Fix Water Colour Seams[/b] [i](client)[/i] — colours each water tile by the depth blended across it, as its waves already are, instead of by one corner, removing the hard colour line along the 64 m grid. [font=Courier New]Water Colour Depth Scale[/font] (default 2) sets how soon shading reaches deep water; above 1 it also makes shallow-water waves look taller than the ones boats ride.
[*][b]Fix Non-Item ObjectDB Entries[/b] [i](both)[/i] — drops prefabs that are not items from the game's item list on load; the 2026-09-09 update listed three ([font=Courier New]PropFeastDeepNorth[/font], [font=Courier New]SnowRoller[/font], [font=Courier New]FrozenKing_Summon[/font]) that break mods treating every entry as an item. They can still be spawned.
[/list]

The two log fixes redirect rather than delete: turn on [font=Courier New]EnableDebugMode[/font] and the messages come back.

[line]

[size=5][b]Credit and sources[/b][/size]

This mod fixes vanilla defects, and other modders found — and in several cases already fixed — a good number of them first. Where that is true it is recorded below, and in a comment at the top of the patch file.

The mods involved:

[list]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/redseiko/ComfyMods]ComfyMods[/url][/b] — redseiko (GPL-3.0), the same licence as this project. Seven of its mods — BetterZeeLog, LetMePlay, BetterServerPortals, Scenic, Compress, Effectual and Atlas — account for eleven of the entries below.
[*][b][url=https://github.com/ontrigger/ValheimPerformanceOptimizations]ValheimPerformanceOptimizations[/url][/b] — ontrigger (MIT). Independent corroboration of one of the performance entries below.
[*][b][url=https://github.com/AzumattDev/MyPitsDontLeak]MyPitsDontLeak[/url][/b] — Azumatt (MIT).
[*][b]Zen.ModLib[/b] — ZenDragon. Used as a reference; no code was used.
[*][b]Iron Gate Studio[/b] — Valheim itself. The decompiled game source is the reference used to locate defects; no game code is redistributed.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Performance[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Fix Portal Connection Scan[/b] — ComfyMods, BetterServerPortals: the indexing algorithm.
[*][b]Fix World Load Connection Scan[/b] — ComfyMods, Atlas: its [font=Courier New]ConnectSpawners[/font] approach, extended here to portals and sync transforms.
[*][b]Fix Tar Pit Buffer Disposal[/b] — MyPitsDontLeak, Azumatt: the root cause; our implementation is a transpiler rather than wholesale method replacement.
[*][b]Fix ZDO Packet Allocation[/b] — ComfyMods, Compress: the technique, taken on its own without that mod's GZip protocol change.
[*][b]Fix Support Lookup Cost[/b] — MidnightsFX, corroborated by ontrigger's ValheimPerformanceOptimizations (MIT): arrived at the same map-probe and lazy-default forms, and the single-fetch centre of mass.
[*][b]Fix Cooking Slot Keys[/b] — Iron Gate Studio: the key-hash pattern, taken from [font=Courier New]ArmorStand.InitKeys[/font].
[*]Every other performance fix — MidnightsFX.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Terrain[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]All five terrain fixes — MidnightsFX.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Correctness[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Fix Object Unload Crash[/b] — ComfyMods, Scenic: the approach.
[*][b]Tolerate Duplicate ZDOs On Load[/b] — ComfyMods, Atlas: the duplicate-id tolerance.
[*][b]Fix Effect Areas[/b] — ComfyMods, Effectual: both defects; our fix for the dangling reference differs.
[*][b]Fix Fuel And Ore Loss[/b] — Zen.ModLib (catalogue): the root cause; rewritten as prefixes.
[*][b]Share Boss Defeat Keys[/b] — Zen.ModLib (catalogue): the defect and approach; rewritten with one globally registered RPC.
[*][b]Fix Recipe Amount Crash[/b] — Zen.ModLib (catalogue): the defect, rewritten.
[*][b]Fix Spawner Null Prefabs[/b] — ComfyMods, LetMePlay: the same fix.
[*][b]Fix Projectile Rotation Spam[/b] — ComfyMods, BetterZeeLog: the same fix.
[*][b]Fix Send Failure Log Spam[/b] — ComfyMods, BetterZeeLog: the defect; that mod removes the call, this one redirects it.
[*][b]Fix Container Log Spam[/b] — ComfyMods, BetterZeeLog: the defect; that mod removes the calls, this one redirects them.
[*][b]Fix Item Icon Crash[/b] — ComfyMods, LetMePlay: the defect; a smaller fix here that leaves the shared item data alone.
[*][b]Fix Negative Stamina[/b], [b]Fix Dungeon Load Stall[/b], [b]Fix Teleport Ghost Players[/b], [b]Fix Unsaved Client Changes[/b], [b]Fix Water Colour Seams[/b] and [b]Fix Non-Item ObjectDB Entries[/b] — MidnightsFX.
[/list]

[line]

[size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size]

Install with a mod manager, or drop [font=Courier New]ValheimCommunityPatch.dll[/font] into [font=Courier New]BepInEx/plugins[/font].

Requires [url=https://valheim.thunderstore.io/package/denikson/BepInExPack_Valheim/]BepInEx[/url] and [url=https://valheim.thunderstore.io/package/ValheimModding/Jotunn/]Jotunn[/url].

[b]Install it on the server and on every client to get all of it.[/b] It is not required on both: a modded client can join a vanilla server, and a modded server accepts vanilla clients. The mod adds no items, prefabs, recipes or save data, so a world it has touched still loads in vanilla, and the one network message it sends is ignored by anyone who does not have it.

What a one-sided install gets you:

[list]
[*][b]Server only[/b] — every [i](server)[/i] and [i](both)[/i] fix. The [i](client)[/i] fixes are not applied at all: a dedicated server has no local player, so nothing there could ever reach them. The startup log says exactly which counts you got.
[*][b]Client only[/b] — every [i](client)[/i] and [i](both)[/i] fix, for you specifically. The [i](server)[/i] fixes are installed but inert unless you are the one hosting.
[/list]

Two caveats for mixed groups:

[list]
[*]Config values are only synced from the server to clients that have the mod — with a one-sided install each machine uses its own config file.
[*]Several fixes are applied by rewriting the method rather than wrapping it, and those read their config once when the game starts. Their descriptions say so. Changing one of those — including a value the server syncs down mid-session — does not take effect on that machine until it restarts.
[/list]

If both the server and a client have the mod, their versions must match on major and minor; Jotunn refuses the connection otherwise, rather than letting the two sides disagree about behavior.

If a machine really does run headless but should still get the client fixes, set [font=Courier New]Patch Every Side[/font] in the config and restart. This is a last resort — it exists in case the graphics-device check that identifies a dedicated server ever gets it wrong.

[line]

[size=5][b]Running alongside other mods[/b][/size]

Several fixes here are applied by rewriting instructions inside a vanilla method rather than wrapping it, and other mods sometimes rewrite the same methods. All of this mod's rewrites are deliberately scheduled to run [b]after[/b] everyone else's, and each one checks that it found what it expected and leaves the method alone if it did not. So where another mod has already fixed the same defect, that mod's version wins and this one stands down rather than fighting it.

When that happens you will see a line like [i]"found no ZLog.Log calls to redirect, so this fix is inactive"[/i] in the log. That is the mechanism working, not a failure. A real failure looks different — [font=Courier New]fix(es) failed[/font] on the startup line, with the exception above it.

Object unloading follows the same principle. Fix Unload Discovery Scan and Fix Object Unload Crash decide what to unload only after every other mod has had its say, so a mod that keeps an object loaded (SeidrChest's remote chest, for example) still keeps it loaded, and a mod that replaces the unload pass itself takes over from both. When another mod does change what stays loaded, the log says so once with [i]"Unload discovery: another mod changed which objects stay loaded"[/i]; that is also the mechanism working.

Known overlap: [b]ComfyMods BetterZeeLog[/b] fixes three of the same defects (container request logging, "Failed to send data", and the projectile zero-velocity rotation warning). The two are safe to run together, and BetterZeeLog's versions of those three take effect.

[line]

[size=5][b]Reporting a bug[/b][/size]

Issues go to [url=https://github.com/MidnightsFX/Valheim-Community-Patch]GitHub[/url]. Please include your [font=Courier New]LogOutput.log[/font] and the list of other mods you are running.

[line]

[size=5][b]Contributing a fix[/b][/size]

To decide whether a fix belongs here, the test is:

[list]
[*]Does it fix a defect in the game itself?
[*]Does it address a significant performance problem (eg 2-5x slowdown)?
[/list]

If it does not meet these criteria, it will not be accepted. This mod is purely bug and performance fixes.
